`sheriff validate` rebuilds these in memory and compares them with what is committed:

- the index of each domain's header, the header read as it now stands in the domain's files;
- the incoming links;
- the values gathered beside each field (§s-ju5vcanbjph7mgh).

`sheriff validate` reads what stands at each end of every consumes and exempts link:

- a consumes link, for a domain's header at its start and a section of another domain at its end;
- an exempts link, for a node of `project.json` at its start, and a rule, a node of a project's own kind or a section at its end.

`sheriff validate` checks that each RecordType variant agrees with the RecordType it refines, found by the variant's refines link:

- the fields the variant answers to are those the nearest RecordType up its refines links names as choosing it;
- each value it answers to is among the values of its choosing field, collected from the field's one source (§s-5nf87c3e3jg9v7c);
- the variant stands in the part of the specification where its value is declared (§s-xfwuvd3funutmsw);
- the variant declares at least one field that no RecordType up its refines links holds, the fields their fields of type variant hold included;
- at most one field of type variant stands along those links;
- no other variant of the same RecordType answers to the same values.

A variant failing any of these is refused.

`sheriff validate` collects the values of each field of type one of from its one source (§s-xfu2gpzddjnmgks), which is one of these:

- its own list;
- the members of the lists that rules of domains add to it. Each such list is found through its rule's refines link, which leads to the RecordType of contracts holding the field, and the list names that field (§s-gzfq3x8hesj227e);
- the names of the domains;
- the sids of the nodes of the kind it names that stand in the base specification.

It refuses:

- a field holding no value;
- a value found in two lists that rules of domains add to the field;
- a domain's list whose rule refines no RecordType of contracts;
- a domain's list naming a field the RecordType its rule refines does not hold, or a field whose source is another;
- a kind named that the schema does not hold.

`sheriff validate` refuses a copy of the SpecSheriff guidelines or of their schema outside the repository they are written in. A copy is:

- a `spec.json` or a `tools.json` under `specs/_guidelines/`;
- any file under `specs/_schema/` but `project.schema.json`.

`sheriff binding` generates in memory the copies the nodes produce, and compares them with what is committed.

- The copies are the files the technical appendix lists for the types and runtime checks (§s-pvzms7d8kxb8dk2), and those each of the project's own generators prints, run as `sheriff types` runs it (§s-km4x4s26kv7pjze).
- Each copy is compared with what the last commit holds instead while a node it waits for is unchecked (§s-f5rz6b7dhxejjts). Each such node is reported.

It refuses:

- a list of the files for the types and runtime checks naming a file of another extension or a path outside the repository;
- a path a project's generator prints outside the repository or under `specs/`;
- two rules of the stack each defining such a list;
- a name that cannot be generated (§s-suv42g8r3ra2h6b);
- a RecordType holding two fields of type variant, its inherited ones counted.

`sheriff binding` reports a node whose tests or code lag behind it, by the order rule, test, code. The check takes the references to the files from the lists of code and tests in the node's metadata (§s-t383grkn3q68dpf). It learns from the history of the repository when each of those files and the node's text last changed. It reports the node where:

- its text changed later than every file of its tests: the tests lag;
- its text changed later than every file of its code: the code lags;
- its tests changed later than every file of its code: the code may not have caught up with the tests.

Changes made in one commit count as made at once. A renamed file is followed under its earlier names too. The node is reported by its sid, naming what lags. The change is not refused.

A commit that changes code names in its message, by sid, every node the tags on its changed symbols name. `sheriff message` refuses the message where:

- a node a tag on a changed symbol names is not named in it;
- a sid in it names no node;
- a sid in it names a domain's header or a section, which no code implements.

`sheriff review` has a runner judge every commit that changes the system's code. A runner is an agent's CLI, started apart from the session that wrote the code (§s-xr5xxa9cxf2mq5u). The runner is given:

- the commit's message;
- the commit's change, less the files generated from the nodes, each of which is named to it with what it holds;
- each node the message or a changed symbol's tag names, with its text and its reason, a RecordType with its fields, and a node of a project's own kind with the values its kind declares;
- each symbol of the system's code outside its tests the commit changes, whole as the commit leaves it, with its file, its lines and its tags.

The symbols are those `sheriff binding` finds changed (§s-2x58az67bm6cxq2). A symbol's tags are its own or those of the symbol it stands in.

The runner judges the commit against the nodes, and each changed symbol, whole, against the nodes its tags name. It refuses the commit where the code does something no named node states in its text, or leaves out what one requires. It also refuses the commit where a changed symbol:

- does something no node its tags name states in its text;
- carries a tag naming a node it does not implement;
- carries the tag that names no node while it implements the system's logic.

A finding of the last kind names as its node the rule that every symbol carries a tag (§s-kgbzk2ph9e6jfb4).

`sheriff review` hands the reviewer's input to a runner. The runner's CLI is given the schema of the verdict and answers in JSON held to it. The verdict passes or refuses the commit and names the findings, each with its node, file, line and problem. `sheriff review` reads an answer that passes the commit with a finding, or refuses it with none, as an answer that cannot be read (§s-nyh9yk22ba6g77q).

A runner is started:

- with none of the user's settings, memory or hooks;
- with a system prompt of its own;
- in an empty working directory;
- with every tool its command line can turn off turned off;
- as a program, never through a shell.

A CLI installed as a command script, as npm installs it on Windows, is started as the program and the arguments the script names.

A runner's environment holds only the variables that name:

- the search path of programs, the folders of the operating system and of the user, and the user's name;
- the locale;
- a proxy, the hosts it skips and the certificates to trust;
- where the runner's CLI keeps its sign-in, the sign-in itself, and the provider serving the model.

The runner `claude` is the Claude Code CLI in print mode. It asks for the model and the reasoning effort the settings below give. Where that model is overloaded or not offered, it falls back to the fallback model they give.

The runner `codex` is the Codex CLI in `codex exec`, with a Codex home of its own. That home is made in a temporary directory for the run and deleted after it, and holds a copy of the user's sign-in and nothing else. Its sandbox is read-only, its session is not kept and no skill is offered. It asks for the model and the reasoning effort the settings below give, and for no model where the model is empty.

`sheriff review` reads the settings of each runner from the git configuration of the user who commits:

- `specsheriff.review.claudeModel`, `specsheriff.review.claudeFallbackModel` and `specsheriff.review.claudeEffort` for the runner `claude`;
- `specsheriff.review.codexModel` and `specsheriff.review.codexEffort` for the runner `codex`, where an empty `codexModel` stands for the model Codex chooses for the signed-in account.

A setting the configuration holds, empty or not, is confirmed. The hooks and the skills of either plugin write a setting only as the user whose configuration it is answers, the default written as any other value. They ask for no setting the configuration holds. The session start (§s-477gtdrkw34dzxw) and the skill `spec-init` (§s-zh2vcr3xjumppg5, §s-2pdy9gw2jrjvfb4) say when each is asked for.

The defaults are:

- for the runner `claude`, the model `opus` at the reasoning effort `high`, falling back to `sonnet`;
- for the runner `codex`, the model Codex chooses, at the reasoning effort `high`.

Where the configuration lacks a setting of the runner that runs, `sheriff review` takes that setting's default. With every review held so, it names each setting it took a default for and how the user confirms it.

The settings of the reviewer in `sheriff.config.json` are `review.runner` and `review.minutes`. `review.minutes` is the time after which a run is ended, twenty minutes unless set. `sheriff review` takes no model and no effort from that file. Unless `review.runner` is set, the runner is that of the plugin whose tools run `sheriff review`. The `agent` file the build writes beside `release` names it: `claude` for the plugin for Claude Code and `codex` for the plugin for Codex CLI. Tools holding no such file take `claude`.
